  "id": "COMPOUND:550 - DISEASE:886",
  "verbalization": "Carbamazepine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Double Outlet Right Ventricle (DORV) through a mechanism involving microRNA 3169. The evidence suggests that carbamazepine affects microRNA 3169, which in turn influences the development of DORV. This is supported by multiple paths showing a direct causal link between carbamazepine and DORV, with microRNA 3169 acting as a central mediator. The microRNA 3169 is the gene product of the Low affinity immunoglobulin gamma Fc region receptor II-c, and its role in causing DORV is consistent across different paths. The observed scores indicate a strong, albeit variable, level of confidence in this relationship. The mechanism appears to be biologically plausible, as microRNAs are known to regulate gene expression and contribute to developmental disorders. However, the exact role of microRNA 3169 in DORV remains to be fully elucidated, and further studies are needed to confirm the therapeutic potential of carbamazepine in this context.",
